<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heart of Darkness</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heart of Darkness is a novella by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-conrad">Joseph Conrad</a>, published in serial form in 1899 and as a book in 1902. Narrated by the seaman Marlow, it describes a journey up an African river to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> agent Kurtz, drawing on Conrad's own voyage up the Congo in 1890. It is among the most widely read and debated works on European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heart-of-darkness.jpg" alt="Heart of Darkness" width="320" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Blackwood (Life time: November 20, 1776 – September 16, 1834) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blackwood%27s_Magazine_-_1899_cover.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-conrad">Joseph Conrad</a> was appointed by a Belgian trading company to serve on one of its steamers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. The captain fell ill and Conrad took command, guiding the ship up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba River</a> to the company's innermost station, Kindu, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. He began to write eight years later. The tale appeared in three parts in Blackwood's Magazine in February, March and April 1899, the February number being the magazine's thousandth issue. It was collected in Youth, A Narrative; and Two Other Stories, published by William Blackwood on 13 November 1902. More than twenty models have been proposed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> agent Kurtz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The story opens on the Thames, where Charles Marlow tells his friends how he became captain of a river steamboat for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> company. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> he finds a station where dying labourers lie near a railway under construction, and he hears of Kurtz, a first-class agent in charge of a distant trading post. Marlow's own steamboat is wrecked, and months of repairs follow before the two-month journey upriver.</p><p>At the Inner Station a Russian wanderer tells him that Kurtz is worshipped by the local people and is gravely ill. Marlow sees severed heads on posts around the station. Kurtz is carried aboard but dies on the way downriver, whispering the words The horror! The horror! Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Marlow visits Kurtz's fiancée and tells her that his last word was her name. The river is never named, but the setting is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, then the private colony of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book drew little comment from critics in 1902, and F. R. Leavis later called it a minor work. By the 1960s it was a standard text in many schools and colleges. In a lecture of 1975 the Nigerian novelist Chinua Achebe called it an offensive and deplorable book that dehumanised Africans. His critics reply that he does not separate Marlow's view from Conrad's and that the novella condemns colonial rule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-d-morel">E. D. Morel</a>, who led opposition to Leopold's rule, called it the most powerful thing written on the subject.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a> took an epigraph for The Hollow Men from it in 1925, and Achebe's Things Fall Apart of 1958 answers it. Francis Ford Coppola's film Apocalypse Now of 1979 moves the story to the Vietnam War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heart of Darkness” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the campaign, including Mark Twain with King Leopold’s Soliloquy (1905) and Arthur Conan Doyle with The Crime of the Congo (1909), while Joseph Conrad’s <b>Heart of Darkness</b>, drawing on his own journey up the river in 1890, became one of the most widely read works on European imperialism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
